 ----------\n3124 |         dtype : data-type or ndarray sub-class, optional\n3125 |             Data-type descriptor of the returned view, e.g., float32 or int16.\n3126 |             The default, None, results in the view having the same data-type\n3127 |             as `a`. As with ``ndarray.view``, dtype can also be specified as\n3128 |             an ndarray sub-class, which then specifies the type of the\n3129 |             returned object (this is equivalent to setting the ``type``\n3130 |             parameter).\n3131 |         type : Python type, optional\n3132 |             Type of the returned view, either ndarray or a subclass.  The\n3133 |             default None results in type preservation.\n3134 |         fill_value : scalar, optional\n3135 |             The value to use for invalid entries (None by default).\n3136 |             If None, then this argument is inferred from the passed `dtype`, or\n3137 |             in its absence the original array, as discussed in the notes below.\n3138 | \n3139 |         See Also\n3140 |         --------\n3141 |         numpy.ndarray.view : Equivalent method on ndarray object.\n3142 | \n3143 |         Notes\n3144 |         -----\n3145 | \n3146 |         ``a.view()`` is used two different ways:\n3147 | \n3148 |         ``a.view(some_dtype)`` or ``a.view(dtype=some_dtype)`` constructs a view\n3149 |         of the array's memory with a different data-type.  This can cause a\n3150 |         reinterpretation of the bytes of memory.\n3151 | \n3152 |         ``a.view(ndarray_subclass)`` or ``a.view(type=ndarray_subclass)`` just\n3153 |         returns an instance of `ndarray_subclass` that looks at the same array\n3154 |         (same shape, dtype, etc.)  This does not cause a reinterpretation of the\n3155 |         memory.\n3156 | \n3157 |         If `fill_value` is not specified, but `dtype` is specified (and is not\n3158 |         an ndarray sub-class), the `fill_value` of the MaskedArray will be\n3159 |         reset. If neither `fill_value` nor `dtype` are specified (or if\n3160 |         `dtype` is an ndarray sub-class), then the fill value is preserved.\n3161 |         Finally, if `fill_value` is specified, but `dtype` is not, the fill\n3162 |         value is set to the specified value.\n3163 | \n3164 |         For ``a.view(some_dtype)``, if ``some_dtype`` has a different number of\n3165 |         bytes per entry than the previous dtype (for example, converting a\n3166 |         regular array to a structured array), then the behavior of the view\n3167 |         cannot be predicted just from the superficial appearance of ``a`` (shown\n3168 |         by ``print(a)``). It also depends on exactly how ``a`` is stored in\n3169 |         memory. Therefore if ``a`` is C-ordered versus fortran-ordered, versus\n3170 |         defined as a slice or transpose, etc., the view may give different\n3171 |         results.\n3172 |         \"\"\"\n3173 | \n3174 |         if dtype is None:\n3175 |             if type is None:\n3176 |                 output = ndarray.view(self)\n3177 |             else:\n3178 |                 output = ndarray.view(self, type)\n3179 |         elif type is None:\n3180 |             try:\n3181 |                 if issubclass(dtype, ndarray):\n3182 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n3183 |                     dtype = None\n3184 |                 else:\n3185 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n3186 |             except TypeError:\n3187 |                 output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n3188 |         else:\n3189 |             output = ndarray.view(self, dtype, type)\n3190 | \n3191 |         # also make the mask be a view (so attr changes to the view's\n3192 |         # mask do no affect original object's mask)\n3193 |         # (especially important to avoid affecting np.masked singleton)\n3194 |         if getmask(output) is not nomask:\n3195 |             output._mask = output._mask.view()\n3196 | \n3197 |         # Make sure to reset the _fill_value if needed\n3198 |         if getattr(output, '_fill_value', None) is not None:\n3199 |             if fill_value is None:\n3200 |                 if dtype is None:\n3201 |                     pass  # leave _fill_value as is\n3202 |                 else:\n3203 |                     output._fill_value = None\n3204 |             else:\n3205 |                 output.fill_value = fill_value\n3206 |         return output\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/ma/mrecords.py:MaskedRecords.view`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `view`.\n```\n361 | def view(self, dtype=None, type=None):\n362 |         \"\"\"\n363 |         Returns a view of the mrecarray.\n364 | \n365 |         \"\"\"\n366 |         # OK, basic copy-paste from MaskedArray.view.\n367 |         if dtype is None:\n368 |             if type is None:\n369 |                 output = ndarray.view(self)\n370 |             else:\n371 |                 output = ndarray.view(self, type)\n372 |         # Here again.\n373 |         elif type is None:\n374 |             try:\n375 |                 if issubclass(dtype, ndarray):\n376 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n377 |                     dtype = None\n378 |                 else:\n379 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n380 |             # OK, there's the change\n381 |             except TypeError:\n382 |                 dtype = np.dtype(dtype)\n383 |                 # we need to revert to MaskedArray, but keeping the possibility\n384 |                 # of subclasses (eg, TimeSeriesRecords), so we'll force a type\n385 |                 # set to the first parent\n386 |                 if dtype.fields is None:\n387 |                     basetype = self.__class__.__bases__[0]\n388 |                     output = self.__array__().view(dtype, basetype)\n389 |                     output._update_from(self)\n390 |                 else:\n391 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n392 |                 output._fill_value = None\n393 |         else:\n394 |             output = ndarray.view(self, dtype, type)\n395 |         # Update the mask, just like in MaskedArray.view\n396 |         if (getattr(output, '_mask', nomask) is not nomask):\n397 |             mdtype = ma.make_mask_descr(output.dtype)\n398 |             output._mask = self._mask.view(mdtype, ndarray)\n399 |             output._mask.shape = output.shape\n400 |         return output\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"shape\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/core/fromnumeric.py:shape`.\nFound function `numpy/core/_internal.py:_ctypes.shape`.\nFound function `numpy/ma/core.py:MaskedArray.shape`.\nFound function `numpy/ma/core.py:shape`.\nFound function `numpy/lib/arrayterator.py:Arrayterator.shape`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `shape`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"axis\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `numpy/fft/_pocketfft.py:_raw_fft`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  45 | # `inv_norm` is a float by which the result of the transform needs to be\n  46 | # divided. This replaces the original, more intuitive 'fct` parameter to avoid\n  47 | # divisions by zero (or alternatively additional checks) in the case of\n  48 | # zero-length axes during its computation.\n  49 | def _raw_fft(a, n, axis, is_real, is_forward, inv_norm):\n  50 |     axis = normalize_axis_index(axis, a.ndim)\n  51 |     if n is None:\n  52 |         n = a.shape[axis]\n  53 | \n  54 |     fct = 1/inv_norm\n  55 | \n  56 |     if a.shape[axis] != n:\n  57 |         s = list(a.shape)\n  58 |         index = [slice(None)]*len(s)\n  59 |         if s[axis] > n:\n  60 |             index[axis] = slice(0, n)\n  61 |             a = a[tuple(index)]\n  62 |         else:\n  63 |             index[axis] = slice(0, s[axis])\n  64 |             s[axis] = n\n  65 |             z = zeros(s, a.dtype.char)\n  66 |             z[tuple(index)] = a\n  67 |             a = z\n  68 | \n  69 |     if axis == a.ndim-1:\n  70 |         r = pfi.execute(a, is_real, is_forward, fct)\n  71 |     else:\n  72 |         a = swapaxes(a, axis, -1)\n  73 |         r = pfi.execute(a, is_real, is_forward, fct)\n  74 |         r = swapaxes(r, axis, -1)\n  75 |     return r\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/linalg/linalg.py:norm`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2363 | def norm(x, ord=None, axis=None, keepdims=False):\n2364 |     \"\"\"\n2365 |     Matrix or vector norm.\n2366 | \n2367 |     This function is able to return one of eight different matrix norms,\n2368 |     or one of an infinite number of vector norms (described below), depending\n2369 |     on the value of the ``ord`` parameter.\n2370 | \n2371 |     Parameters\n2372 |     ----------\n2373 |     x : array_like\n2374 |         Input array.  If `axis` is None, `x` must be 1-D or 2-D, unless `ord`\n2375 |         is None. If both `axis` and `ord` are None, the 2-norm of\n2376 |         ``x.ravel`` will be returned.\n2377 |     ord : {non-zero int, inf, -inf, 'fro', 'nuc'}, optional\n2378 |         Order of the norm (see table under ``Notes``). inf means numpy's\n2379 |         `inf` object. The default is None.\n2380 |     axis : {None, int, 2-tuple of ints}, optional.\n2381 |         If `axis` is an integer, it specifies the axis of `x` along which to\n2382 |         compute the vector norms.  If `axis` is a 2-tuple, it specifies the\n2383 |         axes that hold 2-D matrices, and the matrix norms of these matrices\n2384 |         are computed.  If `axis` is None then either a vector norm (when `x`\n2385 |         is 1-D) or a matrix norm (when `x` is 2-D) is returned. The default\n2386 |         is None.\n2387 | \n2388 |         .. versionadded:: 1.8.0\n2389 | \n2390 |     keepdims : bool, optional\n2391 |         If this is set to True, the axes which are normed over are left in the\n2392 |         result as dimensions with size one.  With this option the result will\n2393 |         broadcast correctly against the original `x`.\n2394 | \n2395 |         .. versionadded:: 1.10.0\n2396 | \n2397 |     Returns\n2398 |     -------\n2399 |     n : float or ndarray\n2400 |         Norm of the matrix or vector(s).\n2401 | \n2402 |     See Also\n2403 |     --------\n2404 |     scipy.linalg.norm : Similar function in SciPy.\n2405 | \n2406 |     Notes\n2407 |     -----\n2408 |     For values of ``ord < 1``, the result is, strictly speaking, not a\n2409 |     mathematical 'norm', but it may still be useful for various numerical\n2410 |     purposes.\n2411 | \n2412 |     The following norms can be calculated:\n2413 | \n2414 |     =====  ============================  ==========================\n2415 |     ord    norm for matrices             norm for vectors\n2416 |     =====  ============================  ==========================\n2417 |     None   Frobenius norm                2-norm\n2418 |     'fro'  Frobenius norm                --\n2419 |     'nuc'  nuclear norm                  --\n2420 |     inf    max(sum(abs(x), axis=1))      max(abs(x))\n2421 |     -inf   min(sum(abs(x), axis=1))      min(abs(x))\n2422 |     0      --                            sum(x != 0)\n2423 |     1      max(sum(abs(x), axis=0))      as below\n2424 |     -1     min(sum(abs(x), axis=0))      as below\n2425 |     2      2-norm (largest sing. value)  as below\n2426 |     -2     smallest singular value       as below\n2427 |     other  --                            sum(abs(x)**ord)**(1./ord)\n2428 |     =====  ============================  ==========================\n2429 | \n2430 |     The Frobenius norm is given by [1]_:\n2431 | \n2432 |         :math:`||A||_F = [\\\\sum_{i,j} abs(a_{i,j})^2]^{1/2}`\n2433 | \n2434 |     The nuclear norm is the sum of the singular values.\n2435 | \n2436 |     Both the Frobenius and nuclear norm orders are only defined for\n2437 |     matrices and raise a ValueError when ``x.ndim != 2``.\n2438 | \n2439 |     References\n2440 |     ----------\n2441 |     .. [1] G. H. Golub and C. F. Van Loan, *Matrix Computations*,\n2442 |            Baltimore, MD, Johns Hopkins University Press, 1985, pg. 15\n2443 | \n2444 |     Examples\n2445 |     --------\n2446 |     >>> from numpy import linalg as LA\n2447 |     >>> a = np.arange(9) - 4\n2448 |     >>> a\n2449 |     array([-4, -3, -2, ...,  2,  3,  4])\n2450 |     >>> b = a.reshape((3, 3))\n2451 |     >>> b\n2452 |     array([[-4, -3, -2],\n2453 |            [-1,  0,  1],\n2454 |            [ 2,  3,  4]])\n2455 | \n2456 |     >>> LA.norm(a)\n2457 |     7.745966692414834\n2458 |     >>> LA.norm(b)\n2459 |     7.745966692414834\n2460 |     >>> LA.norm(b, 'fro')\n2461 |     7.745966692414834\n2462 |     >>> LA.norm(a, np.inf)\n2463 |     4.0\n2464 |     >>> LA.norm(b, np.inf)\n2465 |     9.0\n2466 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -np.inf)\n2467 |     0.0\n2468 |     >>> LA.norm(b, -np.inf)\n2469 |     2.0\n2470 | \n2471 |     >>> LA.norm(a, 1)\n2472 |     20.0\n2473 |     >>> LA.norm(b, 1)\n2474 |     7.0\n2475 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -1)\n2476 |     -4.6566128774142013e-010\n2477 |     >>> LA.norm(b, -1)\n2478 |     6.0\n2479 |     >>> LA.norm(a, 2)\n2480 |     7.745966692414834\n2481 |     >>> LA.norm(b, 2)\n2482 |     7.3484692283495345\n2483 | \n2484 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -2)\n2485 |     0.0\n2486 |     >>> LA.norm(b, -2)\n2487 |     1.8570331885190563e-016 # may vary\n2488 |     >>> LA.norm(a, 3)\n2489 |     5.8480354764257312 # may vary\n2490 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -3)\n2491 |     0.0\n2492 | \n2493 |     Using the `axis` argument to compute vector norms:\n2494 | \n2495 |     >>> c = np.array([[ 1, 2, 3],\n2496 |     ...               [-1, 1, 4]])\n2497 |     >>> LA.norm(c, axis=0)\n2498 |     array([ 1.41421356,  2.23606798,  5.        ])\n2499 |     >>> LA.norm(c, axis=1)\n2500 |     array([ 3.74165739,  4.24264069])\n2501 |     >>> LA.norm(c, ord=1, axis=1)\n2502 |     array([ 6.,  6.])\n2503 | \n2504 |     Using the `axis` argument to compute matrix norms:\n2505 | \n2506 |     >>> m = np.arange(8).reshape(2,2,2)\n2507 |     >>> LA.norm(m, axis=(1,2))\n2508 |     array([  3.74165739,  11.22497216])\n2509 |     >>> LA.norm(m[0, :, :]), LA.norm(m[1, :, :])\n2510 |     (3.7416573867739413, 11.224972160321824)\n2511 | \n2512 |     \"\"\"\n2513 |     x = asarray(x)\n2514 | \n2515 |     if not issubclass(x.dtype.type, (inexact, object_)):\n2516 |         x = x.astype(float)\n2517 | \n2518 |     # Immediately handle some default, simple, fast, and common cases.\n2519 |     if axis is None:\n2520 |         ndim = x.ndim\n2521 |         if ((ord is None) or\n2522 |             (ord in ('f', 'fro') and ndim == 2) or\n2523 |             (ord == 2 and ndim == 1)):\n2524 | \n2525 |             x = x.ravel(order='K')\n2526 |             if isComplexType(x.dtype.type):\n2527 |                 sqnorm = dot(x.real, x.real) + dot(x.imag, x.imag)\n2528 |             else:\n2529 |                 sqnorm = dot(x, x)\n2530 |             ret = sqrt(sqnorm)\n2531 |             if keepdims:\n2532 |                 ret = ret.reshape(ndim*[1])\n2533 |             return ret\n2534 | \n2535 |     # Normalize the `axis` argument to a tuple.\n2536 |     nd = x.ndim\n2537 |     if axis is None:\n2538 |         axis = tuple(range(nd))\n2539 |     elif not isinstance(axis, tuple):\n2540 |         try:\n2541 |             axis = int(axis)\n2542 |         except Exception as e:\n2543 |             raise TypeError(\"'axis' must be None, an integer or a tuple of integers\") from e\n2544 |         axis = (axis,)\n2545 | \n2546 |     if len(axis) == 1:\n2547 |         if ord == Inf:\n2548 |             return abs(x).max(axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2549 |         elif ord == -Inf:\n2550 |             return abs(x).min(axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2551 |         elif ord == 0:\n2552 |             # Zero norm\n2553 |             return (x != 0).astype(x.real.dtype).sum(axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2554 |         elif ord == 1:\n2555 |             # special case for speedup\n2556 |             return add.reduce(abs(x), axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2557 |         elif ord is None or ord == 2:\n2558 |             # special case for speedup\n2559 |             s = (x.conj() * x).real\n2560 |             return sqrt(add.reduce(s, axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims))\n2561 |         # None of the str-type keywords for ord ('fro', 'nuc')\n2562 |         # are valid for vectors\n2563 |         elif isinstance(ord, str):\n2564 |             raise ValueError(f\"Invalid norm order '{ord}' for vectors\")\n2565 |         else:\n2566 |             absx = abs(x)\n2567 |             absx **= ord\n2568 |             ret = add.reduce(absx, axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2569 |             ret **= (1 / ord)\n2570 |             return ret\n2571 |     elif len(axis) == 2:\n2572 |         row_axis, col_axis = axis\n2573 |         row_axis = normalize_axis_index(row_axis, nd)\n2574 |         col_axis = normalize_axis_index(col_axis, nd)\n2575 |         if row_axis == col_axis:\n2576 |             raise ValueError('Duplicate axes given.')\n2577 |         if ord == 2:\n2578 |             ret =  _multi_svd_norm(x, row_axis, col_axis, amax)\n2579 |         elif ord == -2:\n2580 |             ret = _multi_svd_norm(x, row_axis, col_axis, amin)\n2581 |         elif ord == 1:\n2582 |             if col_axis > row_axis:\n2583 |                 col_axis -= 1\n2584 |             ret = add.reduce(abs(x), axis=row_axis).max(axis=col_axis)\n2585 |         elif ord == Inf:\n2586 |             if row_axis > col_axis:\n2587 |                 row_axis -= 1\n2588 |             ret = add.reduce(abs(x), axis=col_axis).max(axis=row_axis)\n2589 |         elif ord == -1:\n2590 |             if col_axis > row_axis:\n2591 |                 col_axis -= 1\n2592 |             ret = add.reduce(abs(x), axis=row_axis).min(axis=col_axis)\n2593 |         elif ord == -Inf:\n2594 |             if row_axis > col_axis:\n2595 |                 row_axis -= 1\n2596 |             ret = add.reduce(abs(x), axis=col_axis).min(axis=row_axis)\n2597 |         elif ord in [None, 'fro', 'f']:\n2598 |             ret = sqrt(add.reduce((x.conj() * x).real, axis=axis))\n2599 |         elif ord == 'nuc':\n2600 |             ret = _multi_svd_norm(x, row_axis, col_axis, sum)\n2601 |         else:\n2602 |             raise ValueError(\"Invalid norm order for matrices.\")\n2603 |         if keepdims:\n2604 |             ret_shape = list(x.shape)\n2605 |             ret_shape[axis[0]] = 1\n2606 |             ret_shape[axis[1]] = 1\n2607 |             ret = ret.reshape(ret_shape)\n2608 |         return ret\n2609 |     else:\n2610 |         raise ValueError(\"Improper number of dimensions to norm.\")\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/core/numeric.py:normalize_axis_tuple`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1341 | def normalize_axis_tuple(axis, ndim, argname=None, allow_duplicate=False):\n1342 |     \"\"\"\n1343 |     Normalizes an axis argument into a tuple of non-negative integer axes.\n1344 | \n1345 |     This handles shorthands such as ``1`` and converts them to ``(1,)``,\n1346 |     as well as performing the handling of negative indices covered by\n1347 |     `normalize_axis_index`.\n1348 | \n1349 |     By default, this forbids axes from being specified multiple times.\n1350 | \n1351 |     Used internally by multi-axis-checking logic.\n1352 | \n1353 |     .. versionadded:: 1.13.0\n1354 | \n1355 |     Parameters\n1356 |     ----------\n1357 |     axis : int, iterable of int\n1358 |         The un-normalized index or indices of the axis.\n1359 |     ndim : int\n1360 |         The number of dimensions of the array that `axis` should be normalized\n1361 |         against.\n1362 |     argname : str, optional\n1363 |         A prefix to put before the error message, typically the name of the\n1364 |         argument.\n1365 |     allow_duplicate : bool, optional\n1366 |         If False, the default, disallow an axis from being specified twice.\n1367 | \n1368 |     Returns\n1369 |     -------\n1370 |     normalized_axes : tuple of int\n1371 |         The normalized axis index, such that `0 <= normalized_axis < ndim`\n1372 | \n1373 |     Raises\n1374 |     ------\n1375 |     AxisError\n1376 |         If any axis provided is out of range\n1377 |     ValueError\n1378 |         If an axis is repeated\n1379 | \n1380 |     See also\n1381 |     --------\n1382 |     normalize_axis_index : normalizing a single scalar axis\n1383 |     \"\"\"\n1384 |     # Optimization to speed-up the most common cases.\n1385 |     if type(axis) not in (tuple, list):\n1386 |         try:\n1387 |             axis = [operator.index(axis)]\n1388 |         except TypeError:\n1389 |             pass\n1390 |     # Going via an iterator directly is slower than via list comprehension.\n1391 |     axis = tuple([normalize_axis_index(ax, ndim, argname) for ax in axis])\n1392 |     if not allow_duplicate and len(set(axis)) != len(axis):\n1393 |         if argname:\n1394 |             raise ValueError('repeated axis in `{}` argument'.format(argname))\n1395 |         else:\n1396 |             raise ValueError('repeated axis')\n1397 |     return axis\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/core/numeric.py:_moveaxis_dispatcher`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1400 | def _moveaxis_dispatcher(a, source, destination):\n1401 |     return (a,)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/core/numeric.py:_move_axis_to_0`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1477 | def _move_axis_to_0(a, axis):\n1478 |     return moveaxis(a, axis, 0)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/ma/extras.py:apply_along_axis`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n372 | def apply_along_axis(func1d, axis, arr, *args, **kwargs):\n373 |     \"\"\"\n374 |     (This docstring should be overwritten)\n375 |     \"\"\"\n376 |     arr = array(arr, copy=False, subok=Tru